{% comment %}
  The site's logo as an independent, page-level element — a sibling of the
  header and main content (rendered directly in layout/theme.liquid), not
  nested inside any section. This is deliberate: a logo tied to a section's
  DOM disappears if that section is ever removed/reordered, and — for the
  home page specifically — needs to sit at a fixed viewport position that
  survives past the hero section's own scroll range, which a descendant of
  that section can't do on its own.

  Renders "docked" (small, fixed near the top) by default everywhere. On
  the home page, hero-scroll.js finds this element via [data-site-logo],
  gsap.set()s it to a large centered override the moment the page loads,
  then scrubs it back to identity (i.e. this docked resting state) as the
  hero plays out — so no separate "landed" class or position-mode switch is
  needed; the resting values here ARE the landing target.

  The native width is deliberately huge (2000px) with the docked look coming
  entirely from `scale(0.1)` — never the other way around. A GPU compositing
  layer's raster is sized to its pre-transform layout box, so an element
  that's natively 200px and gets scaled *up* for the hero's big state is
  visibly blurry (a 200px raster stretched ~4–5x). Rendering natively large
  and always scaling *down* avoids that; hero-scroll.js's opening-state
  scale is calibrated against this same 2000px, and must stay in sync with
  it if this ever changes.

  This fully replaces the theme's own header-logo block (see the global
  override in this file's stylesheet) — simpler than trying to hide it only
  on some templates.
{% endcomment %}
<a
  href="{{ routes.root_url }}"
  class="site-logo"
  data-site-logo
>
  {% render 'logo-wordmark' %}
</a>

{% stylesheet %}
  .site-logo {
    position: fixed;
    top: 33px;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 2000px;
    transform: scale(0.1);
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
    z-index: 1000;
    display: block;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  .site-logo svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /*
   * No colour or blend of its own. Sitting inside .header-section, it is
   * painted into the group that element blends, and the header's own colour
   * rules name .site-logo alongside .header__row — so it inverts, turns the
   * light-section colour, and takes the drawer's colour without any of that
   * being restated here.
   */
  .site-logo {
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }

  /* This snippet is the single source of truth for the logo everywhere —
     the theme's own header-logo block is redundant now, on every template
     (not just the home page), so it's switched off globally here rather
     than fought with per-template visibility settings. */
  .header-logo {
    display: none !important; /* stylelint-disable-line declaration-no-important */
  }

  /* The theme's transparent+sticky header intentionally goes solid white
     once "stuck" (see sections/header.liquid's [data-sticky-state] rules).
     We want it to stay see-through everywhere instead, since .site-logo —
     not the header — carries the brand mark now. */
  #header-component,
  .header__underlay-closed,
  .header__underlay-open {
    background: transparent !important; /* stylelint-disable-line declaration-no-important */
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
